\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\ul\b\f0\fs24\'b6 Verse 1\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 1. How blessed, from the bonds of sin
\par And earthly fetters free,
\par In singleness of heart and aim,
\par Thy servant, Lord, to be;
\par The hardest toil to undertake
\par With joy at Thy command,
\par The meanest office to receive
\par With meekness at Thy hand;
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 2\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 2. With willing heart and longing eyes
\par To watch before Thy gate,
\par Ready to run the weary race,
\par To bear the heavy weight;
\par No voice of thunder to expect,
\par But follow, calm and still:
\par For love can easily divine
\par The One Beloved's will.
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 3\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 3. Thus may I serve Thee, gracious Lord,
\par Thus ever Thine alone,
\par My soul and body given to Thee,
\par The purchase Thou hast won;
\par Through evil or through good report,
\par Still keeping by Thy side:
\par By life or death, in this poor flesh,
\par Let Christ be magnified.
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 4\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 4. How happily the working days
\par In this dear service fly !
\par How rapidly the closing hour,
\par The time of rest, draws nigh,
\par When all the faithful gather home,
\par A joyful company,
\par And ever where the Master is
\par Shall His blest servants be.\f1 
\par \cf1\ul\b\fs16 
\par }

